  author-tack-profiles[2m  tack · builtin · opt-in · user default[22m
    Author, publish, inspect and repair fixed Tack profiles. Use
    for profile files, source linkage, independent membership
    copies, explicit application and portable content bundles.
    Use manage-tack-sources for resource declarations and
    use-tack for daily selection.
  manage-tack-sources[2m  tack · builtin · opt-in · user default[22m
    Author Tack sources, folders of skills, commands, context,
    scripts and docs. Use before editing any file inside a
    source, to check its maintainers first, and to create or
    register a source, declare resources in tack.yaml, set
    dependencies, resolve name clashes or fix catalog issues.
  prepare-tack-checkout[2m  tack · builtin · opt-in · user default[22m
    Link a repository's Tack skills and docs before you work in
    it. Use at the start of work in a repository whose AGENTS.md
    or CLAUDE.md has a Tack section, or whose root has a
    tack.yaml, and in every new clone or worktree of one. Use
    use-tack for any other Tack command.
  use-tack[2m  tack · builtin · opt-in · user default[22m
    Operate Tack, which manages cross-agent skills, commands,
    context, docs and scripts. Use for any `tack` command -
    discover resources, pick and sync them into Claude Code,
    Codex, Cursor, OpenCode or Pi, attach and search docs, run
    declared scripts, check health and upgrades. To create or
    edit a source, use the `manage-tack-sources` skill.
  write-reader-figures[2m  tack · builtin · opt-in · user default[22m
    Write figures in Tack docs - architecture maps, running
    systems, schemas and domain models, file trees,
    walkthroughs, charts and authored figures that the docs
    reader renders with one frame. Use when a document needs a
    picture of how code is organised, which parts run and what
    they send each other, which tables or entities exist and who
    may touch them, what is on disk, an ordered procedure,
    quantities or a one-off mechanism, before writing any figure
    or a `tree` block, and instead of ASCII art. Also for icons,
    callouts, tabs and defined terms in MDX docs. Run `tack
    check` on the page when done.
